-- title:
--   Γ(N/p) inside Γ₁(N)∩Γ(M) with a lower unipotent
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $p$ a prime dividing $N$; write $e=v_p(N)$ for the exponent of $p$ in the factorisation of $N$, so that $N/p^{e}$ is the prime-to-$p$ part of $N$ (all divisions being exact natural-number divisions). Inside $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, let $\Gamma(M)$ denote the principal congruence subgroup of matrices congruent to the identity modulo $M$, let $\Gamma_1(N)$ denote the subgroup of matrices whose diagonal entries are congruent to $1$ and whose lower left entry is congruent to $0$ modulo $N$, and let $S=\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$ and $T=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ be the standard generators. The assertion is the inclusion of subgroups
--   $$\Gamma(N/p)\ \le\ \bigl(\Gamma_1(N)\cap\Gamma(N/p^{e})\bigr)\ \vee\ \langle\, S\,T^{N/p}\,S^{-1}\,\rangle,$$
--   where the right-hand side is the join, i.e. the subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ generated by $\Gamma_1(N)\cap\Gamma(N/p^{e})$ together with all integral powers of the element $S\,T^{N/p}\,S^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-N/p&1\end{pmatrix}$. Equivalently: every matrix congruent to the identity modulo $N/p$ is a product of matrices lying in $\Gamma_1(N)$ and congruent to the identity modulo the prime-to-$p$ part of $N$, and of powers of that lower unipotent matrix.
--
--   This is a global, $S$-conjugated form of the generation lemma of Diamond and Shurman (Lemma 5.7.6), whose local statement concerns upper right congruence conditions at $p^{e}$; conjugating by $S$ turns those into the conditions defining $\Gamma_1$ and produces the lower unipotent $\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-N/p&1\end{pmatrix}$, while the intersection with $\Gamma(N/p^{e})$ keeps every factor trivial modulo the prime-to-$p$ part of $N$. It is used in the Atkin–Lehner theory of cusp forms, through [`CuspForm.exists_sum_eq_forall_gamma1_div_slash_eq`](thm.html#CuspForm.exists_sum_eq_forall_gamma1_div_slash_eq), to show that a form invariant under the listed generators is invariant under all of $\Gamma(N/p)$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CongruenceSubgroup_Gamma_div_le_gamma1_inf_Gamma_sup_zpowers.lean

